1
00:00:00,278 --> 00:00:08,712
This week at Humanitarians AI, I focused on educational video reviews, YouTube publishing materials, and four Claude Bear videos about Claude Skills.

2
00:00:08,898 --> 00:00:12,611
My work involved content from Anjana, Tanmay, and Onkar.

3
00:00:12,796 --> 00:00:15,265
This is Liam, narrating for Deepa.

4
00:00:15,451 --> 00:00:20,527
I reviewed and prepared publishing materials; I did not create the four Claude Bear videos.

5
00:00:21,778 --> 00:00:29,304
I reviewed submissions from Anjana, Tanmay, and Onkar for educational content, presentation quality, and suitability for YouTube.

6
00:00:29,489 --> 00:00:35,277
I checked clarity, organization, visual presentation, and whether viewers could follow the explanation.

7
00:00:35,462 --> 00:00:41,862
I also worked on titles and descriptions, and identified items that might need clarification before publication.

8
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:47,099
This helped me understand why educational quality control matters.

9
00:00:47,284 --> 00:00:53,348
A technically complete video still needs evaluation for readability, consistency, and audience engagement.

10
00:00:53,534 --> 00:00:58,966
Reviewing different contributors’ work also showed me the importance of consistent publishing standards.

11
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:04,227
Technical completion is a starting point, not a complete judgment of educational value.

12
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:13,089
For Web Artifacts Builder, I prepared a YouTube title and structured description covering the skill’s purpose, workflow, benefits, and limitations.

13
00:01:13,274 --> 00:01:17,699
I included a hands-on activity, topic information, and relevant hashtags.

14
00:01:17,884 --> 00:01:24,817
I learned that an AI-built interface does not automatically guarantee code quality, accessibility, or a good user experience.

15
00:01:26,069 --> 00:01:30,316
For Webapp Testing, I prepared the title, description, and viewer exercise.

16
00:01:30,502 --> 00:01:35,598
I focused on automated testing, functionality, user interactions, and validating results.

17
00:01:35,783 --> 00:01:43,368
My lesson was that tests can identify problems efficiently, but a passing result does not guarantee a completely defect-free application.

18
00:01:43,553 --> 00:01:46,141
The choice of test cases still matters.

19
00:01:47,361 --> 00:01:51,746
For XLSX, I reviewed publishing readiness and prepared a title and description.

20
00:01:51,931 --> 00:01:59,615
I checked format and presentation, identified visuals worth verifying, and explained pandas, openpyxl, formulas, and validation.

21
00:01:59,801 --> 00:02:06,339
I learned that spreadsheet automation reduces repetitive work, but formula correctness and business accuracy are different.

22
00:02:06,524 --> 00:02:09,290
Human verification remains important.

23
00:02:10,528 --> 00:02:18,706
For Slack GIF Creator, I prepared a title and description about animation workflows, technical requirements, creative limitations, and a viewer exercise.

24
00:02:18,891 --> 00:02:24,224
I learned that generating an animation successfully does not guarantee effective communication.

25
00:02:24,409 --> 00:02:29,703
Timing, readability, context, and audience interpretation all matter when judging the result.

26
00:02:30,944 --> 00:02:40,110
I organized publishing materials using a consistent format: an introduction, explanation of the skill, practical application, limitations, and a hands-on activity.

27
00:02:40,295 --> 00:02:46,517
The Claude Bear materials included descriptive titles, educational summaries, hashtags, and viewer exercises.

28
00:02:46,702 --> 00:02:50,693
This connected technical details with a clear purpose for the audience.

29
00:02:51,944 --> 00:02:56,488
I contacted Sanjana to clarify the playlist for the Claude Bear submissions.

30
00:02:56,673 --> 00:03:01,868
Claude Basics was my proposed category, while I sought confirmation before finalizing it.

31
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:05,826
I learned to confirm publishing requirements rather than assume them.

32
00:03:06,011 --> 00:03:10,219
This report does not claim playlist approval or that all videos were published.

33
00:03:11,444 --> 00:03:21,242
My takeaways were stronger video-review and quality-assurance skills, experience writing structured publishing materials, and a better understanding of four Claude Skills and their limitations.

34
00:03:21,427 --> 00:03:23,956
I also practiced team coordination.

35
00:03:24,141 --> 00:03:31,153
The common lesson was to distinguish useful AI automation from the human judgment needed to assess an educational outcome.

36
00:03:32,403 --> 00:03:39,751
This week’s work strengthened my understanding of educational content review, AI-assisted workflows, and YouTube publishing standards.

37
00:03:39,936 --> 00:03:49,319
Reviewing team submissions and working with Claude Bear videos gave me practical experience evaluating technical content and communicating it clearly to a broader audience.

38
00:03:49,504 --> 00:03:51,539
Liam, narrating for Deepa.

39
00:03:51,724 --> 00:03:54,904
Humanitarians AI, Weekly Progress Report.
